WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.465
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.800
MICHELLE CASBON: Hi,
and welcome to episode

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:26.070
number 177 of the weekly
"Google Cloud Platform" Podcast.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.860
I'm Michelle, and I'm here with
my colleague, Mark Mirchandani.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:29.650
Hi, Mark.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:30.900
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Hi Michelle.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:31.650
How are you today?

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:32.983
MICHELLE CASBON: I'm doing well.

00:00:32.983 --> 00:00:33.566
How about you?

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:35.400
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I'm
doing very, very good.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.320
And I'd like to take
this chance to call out

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.965
that May, which is the month
that we're currently in,

00:00:39.965 --> 00:00:41.340
is Mental Health
Awareness Month.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.470
MICHELLE CASBON: Mental
Health Awareness Month--

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.070
what exactly does that mean?

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:46.770
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
I have a vague idea

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.910
of what it means, and
through some readings,

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:51.000
and a lot of tweet
conversations, and people

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.220
just kind of promoting
this idea that, hey, it's

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.138
important to take some time and
recognize that sometimes you

00:00:56.138 --> 00:00:57.180
need to take a step back.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:59.263
Sometimes you need to take
some time for yourself.

00:00:59.263 --> 00:01:01.260
I think there's a lot
of negative stereotypes

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.685
about going to therapy.

00:01:02.685 --> 00:01:04.185
And there's a lot
of ideas out there

00:01:04.185 --> 00:01:06.120
that people are starting
to talk about them more.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.900
And I think it's important
to raise awareness of it.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.317
And that's why I
think that having

00:01:09.317 --> 00:01:11.580
a month dedicated
to helping people

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.650
understand what mental
health awareness is

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.390
and why it's so
important for people

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:18.930
to kind of focus in on
not overloading themselves

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:20.770
with work, taking
time for themselves,

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:22.770
setting up an environment
where they feel good--

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.000
all of these kind of things are
really important to focus on.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:26.130
And so that's why it's
kind of cool that there's

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:27.143
a month dedicated to it.

00:01:27.143 --> 00:01:28.560
MICHELLE CASBON:
Yeah, so I really

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.630
like the fact that we
are talking about it,

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:34.020
that we're sort of normalizing
it, and making it clear

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.317
that there's no shame in it.

00:01:35.317 --> 00:01:36.900
It's a way of taking
care of ourselves

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.990
in the same way we take
care of our physical health.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:43.230
One of the best phrasing for
this is from a book by Brené

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.090
Brown, one of my
favorite authors.

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:49.320
So she describes it as if we
are exhaling all the time,

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:50.675
we need to be inhaling.

00:01:50.675 --> 00:01:52.050
And Mental Health
Awareness Month

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.415
is a time for us to inhale,
so that we can exhale.

00:01:54.415 --> 00:01:56.040
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Oh, very interesting.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.460
I'm going to have to
check that book out.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:57.840
MICHELLE CASBON:
It's a good one.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.820
I believe that one was from
"Braving the Wilderness,"

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:01.620
which was one of the most
recent ones that I read.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:02.010
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Gotcha.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.970
Maybe we'll put a link
down in the show notes.

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:04.620
MICHELLE CASBON: Great idea.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:05.703
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Perfect.

00:02:05.703 --> 00:02:07.560
Well, onto the main
content, today,

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.250
we have a very, very,
very, very, very--

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:14.553
I said six very-- seven very
cool interview with-- well,

00:02:14.553 --> 00:02:15.720
why don't you introduce him?

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.700
MICHELLE CASBON: That
was a lot of verys.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.280
So I agree that it was
a fascinating interview

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.350
with our friend John Bohannon.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.870
So he co-presented a
session with me at Next,

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.780
and so we ask him a little
bit about his work, his path

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.700
into data science,
and how he got

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.330
to where he is now, which is a
director of science at Primer,

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:34.827
a startup here in the Bay Area.

00:02:34.827 --> 00:02:36.660
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Which
as a teaser, I mean,

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.580
this interview was wild, right?

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.190
I mean, we heard so
many cool stories.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:44.088
I'm just remembering all the
fun things that we talked about.

00:02:44.088 --> 00:02:45.630
It's a really, really
good interview.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:47.080
Sorry-- a really, really,
really, really, really, really,

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:48.450
really good interview.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.700
MICHELLE CASBON: It was one
of my favorites, for sure.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.440
He's just a fascinating person.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:56.970
But before we get to that, we
have a really exciting question

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.708
of the week that follows up
from our conversation with John.

00:02:59.708 --> 00:03:01.500
MARK MIRCHANDANI: We
do, but even before we

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.083
get to the question
of the week, we're

00:03:03.083 --> 00:03:04.950
going to do our cool
thing of the week.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.686
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:09.054 --> 00:03:12.060
MICHELLE CASBON: Cool thing of
the week-- there are so many.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.450
This is the week right
after Google I/O.

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:16.920
We were both there last week.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.760
We had so much to do.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:20.770
It was an action packed week.

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:23.757
So how do we condense that
into cool things of the week?

00:03:23.757 --> 00:03:26.340
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well, I'm glad
you asked because as it turns

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.142
out, all of the sessions
that happened at I/O,

00:03:29.142 --> 00:03:30.600
and actually, all
the sessions that

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.880
happened at Next, which
wasn't that long ago--

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.970
all of those are available on
YouTube for people to re-watch.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.640
Now there is a ton
of sessions at both.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.130
And as you can imagine,
Google Cloud Next

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.710
had a ton of cloud content.

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:45.480
I/O had some cloud content, but
there was a lot of other talks

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.563
there, especially focusing
on other Google things,

00:03:47.563 --> 00:03:49.480
