Chrome, and so on.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:51.960
So all of those talks
are available on YouTube.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.750
If you go to the GCP Cloud
YouTube channel, as well

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:57.112
as the links we'll probably
put down in the show notes,

00:03:57.112 --> 00:03:59.070
you can check out all
these different sessions.

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:00.445
You can kind of
read through them

00:04:00.445 --> 00:04:03.083
and see what they're about,
and see who was talking,

00:04:03.083 --> 00:04:04.500
and kind of catch
up on the topics

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:05.833
that you think were interesting.

00:04:05.833 --> 00:04:07.660
But I know that
while we were there,

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:09.660
I was hanging out mostly
at the community booth,

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.600
talking with people, looking at
some of the new announcements.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.860
But you had a chance to
actually go to some sessions.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:16.560
So what was your
favorite session?

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:17.940
What was your favorite Code Lab?

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.560
And what was your
favorite Sandbox?

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.435
MICHELLE CASBON: Oh, so
many great questions.

00:04:21.435 --> 00:04:24.210
So if you don't want to go
through all of the videos

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.730
from I/O, if you just
want a shortcut to what

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.100
was my favorite
session by far, that

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:31.650
would have to be the session
where Taylor Wilson interviewed

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:32.640
Michio Kaku.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.040
So he is one of my
favorite scientists.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.110
I've been reading
his books for years.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.570
He's been on so
many PBS programs.

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:41.370
He's a very
public-facing scientist,

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.170
and there's not that
many of them out there.

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:46.065
So he's been pretty
transformational for me.

00:04:46.065 --> 00:04:47.940
And what I really enjoyed
about the session--

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:50.370
it was sort of a
fireside chat format.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:52.020
So Taylor has a lot
in common with him,

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.810
and you could tell that this
scientist, in particular,

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.860
was very formative for
Taylor's early years.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:59.800
And so he was just so excited
to be interviewing him,

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.580
and there was so much
energy in the room.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:04.247
And he asked such
intelligent questions,

00:05:04.247 --> 00:05:06.331
and there was so much
overlap in their backgrounds

00:05:06.331 --> 00:05:08.370
that it was just really
fascinating to watch.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.037
It was not only a
fascinating interview,

00:05:10.037 --> 00:05:12.770
but the interviewer was
also just really on point.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:13.950
So I recommend that session.

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:14.970
It was by far my favorite.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:15.570
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
that would definitely

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:18.180
be one of the great videos
to check out on YouTube.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:19.410
So what about the Sandboxes?

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:21.450
I mean, there are a lot
of really cool demos

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:22.950
out there that
people could actually

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:24.450
get hands-on and
play around with

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:26.408
from, again, a wide
variety of Google products.

00:05:26.408 --> 00:05:28.130
Which ones did you
kind of like the best?

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:29.790
MICHELLE CASBON: My
favorite would be

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:32.190
the one called AI on the Edge.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.290
This was created by a
few of our co-workers.

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:36.710
Gabe Weiss, Noah
Negrey, and Yu-Han Liu,

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:40.500
who are both on my team, and
Gus, or Luis Gustavo Martins--

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:41.640
they all put this together.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:44.310
And the cool thing about
that is the Sandboxes

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:47.850
are physical instantiations
of these projects.

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:52.140
And so it was like a living--
do you remember on "The Price is

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:53.385
Right" the Plinko boards?

00:05:53.385 --> 00:05:54.380
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Oh, absolutely.

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:56.213
Everyone remembers the
Plinko boards, right?

00:05:56.213 --> 00:05:57.085
It's the best game.

00:05:57.085 --> 00:05:58.960
MICHELLE CASBON: So you
have that round token

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:02.485
that sort of makes its way
down and drops into a slot.

00:06:02.485 --> 00:06:04.110
So the cool thing
about this is that it

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:07.770
used TensorFlow Lite to
identify the number that

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:09.840
was on the token, and
then it would drop it down

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.330
into the right slot
according to the number that

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:14.010
was written on the token.

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:15.750
It was super cool.

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:17.550
So there were a
couple parts to this.

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:21.030
There was image recognition
for recognizing the number.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:25.470
And I believe that was
an on-device edge TPU.

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:28.690
And then there was also
the object recognition,

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:30.840
which was an Android app.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:33.120
All of that was using
TensorFlow Lite--

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:35.610
so different types of hardware.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:37.230
And the models that they used--

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.450
digit detection was
taken from AutoML,

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:42.348
and then the object detection
was using Mobilenet.

00:06:42.348 --> 00:06:44.640
And the cool thing about that
is that all the code that

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:46.080
ran that will be open sourced.

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:49.000
Gabe is essentially
opening it this week.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.000
So by the time show comes
out, it should be out.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.375
And you'll be able to see all
the code that went into it,

00:06:53.375 --> 00:06:54.700
everything that went behind it.

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:56.700
And he is also in the
process of writing

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:58.410
a blog post, or several.

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:02.532
So stay tuned on the GCP blog
for a written description

00:07:02.532 --> 00:07:03.990
of everything that
he put together.

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:06.360
So you, too, can feel like
you were at I/O to view

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:07.950
the AI on the Edge Sandbox.

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:09.150
MARK MIRCHANDANI: That's the
best part about it, right?

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:10.830
Because if you didn't
get a chance to come,

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:12.240
the sessions are out on YouTube.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.448
A lot of the code-- hopefully,
not just Gabe's demo--

00:07:14.448 --> 00:07:15.378
are going open source.

00:07:15.378 --> 00:07:16.920
There's still a lot
of hands-on stuff

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:18.420
that you probably
can't replicate,

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:21.090
which is why you should probably
come to the next year's I/O.

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:22.570
But if you weren't
able to make it,

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:24.750
there are lots and lots of cool
ways to catch up with that.

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:27.150
Not only that, but Code Labs
are another great way, right?

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:29.775
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah, so that's
where I spent most of my time,

00:07:29.775 --> 00:07:30.930
in the Code Lab section.

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:34.230
And my favorite lab
was the one that

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:37.170
uses a SparkFun edge board.

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:38.940
So this one also uses
TF Lite, which is

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:40.890
what was powering Gabe's demo.

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:42.810
So it's AI on a
microcontroller with TF Lite.

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:45.150
And I had seen this
a few weeks before

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:47.250
by one of the lead
researchers on the project,

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:48.840
but at I/O was the
first time where

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:49.980
users could come together.

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:51.730
And they had all the
written instructions,

00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:53.350
and they got to do
this themselves.

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:55.380
So it walked them
through, on the board,

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:58.270
creating these models,
putting it onto the board.

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:00.210
And then you could
yell at the board.

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:01.443
It was a speech recognizer.

00:08:01.443 --> 00:08:03.360
And then the right colored
light would go off,

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.277
and so you had throughout
the Code Labs people

00:08:05.277 --> 00:08:07.910
just randomly screaming,
yes, yes, no, yes, no, no!

00:08:07.910 --> 00:08:10.160
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I think I
saw something very similar

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:11.720
with animals, and
people had to scream out

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:14.160
the animal noises, which was
an interesting demo as well.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:15.035
[ELEPHANT TRUMPETING]

00:08:15.035 --> 00:08:17.535
MICHELLE CASBON: That was the
highlight of Code Labs for me,

00:08:17.535 --> 00:08:18.320
was that one lab.

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:19.280
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Oh, that's awesome.

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:21.620
And one of the other cool
things that was at I/O

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:23.990
was the Detox Zone,
and which we were just

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:25.580
talking about mental
health awareness

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:26.750
and how important it is.

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:27.917
MICHELLE CASBON: Absolutely.

00:08:27.917 --> 00:08:31.830
So that was probably my
favorite miscellaneous zone.

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:34.460
I really appreciated that
there was a place for people

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:35.720
to sort of retreat.

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:36.992
What I loved about I/O--

00:08:36.992 --> 00:08:38.450
and it was so
different from Next--

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:40.865
it was more of a developer
festival atmosphere,

00:08:40.865 --> 00:08:42.799
so it was all outdoors.

00:08:42.799 --> 00:08:44.450
And it was much
different from Next.

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:46.100
So Next is in Moscone.

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:48.470
I/O was outdoors,
and you were part

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:49.970
of nature, which I appreciated.

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:51.830
And this digital
detox zone was kind of

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:53.700
off to the side in a corner.

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:55.580
And I think my
favorite part about it

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:59.720
is that there was a woman
at a booth with a typewriter

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:00.890
offering poems.

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:03.170
So you could just walk
up and get a poem.

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:05.160
And she didn't
even use a laptop.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:07.280
And it was literally
just a piece of paper

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:08.300
that she handed to you.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:09.260
It was lovely.

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:11.480
So in the middle of
this detox garden,

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:13.370
it was a fantastic
contrast to the rest

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:15.650
of the sort of overwhelming
festival atmosphere.

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:16.910
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So it's
a really great experience.

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:18.990
Obviously, for people
who do get a chance,

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:20.370
it's super, super awesome.

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:22.512
If you don't, we do our
best to try and get as much

00:09:22.512 --> 00:09:23.720
of that experience out there.

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:25.640
But there probably just
isn't quite any way

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:27.265
to replicate it,
other than showing up.

00:09:27.265 --> 00:09:28.515
MICHELLE CASBON: That's right.

00:09:28.515 --> 00:09:29.390
So come next year.

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:31.065
It was my first I/O,
and I am definitely

00:09:31.065 --> 00:09:31.940
going back next year.

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:32.870
It was fantastic.

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:33.560
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Awesome to hear.

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:35.810
Well, I don't want to delay
our conversation with John

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:36.420
any longer.

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:37.620
So shall we dive into that?

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:39.078
MICHELLE CASBON:
Let's get started.

00:09:39.078 --> 00:09:40.645
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:09:41.949 --> 00:09:42.449
All right.

00:09:42.449 --> 00:09:43.660
Welcome, everyone.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:47.400
We're very happy to have our
special guest today, John

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:49.020
Bohannon, here from Primer.

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:50.790
He's the director
of science there.

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:51.630
JOHN BOHANNON: Hi.

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:53.630
MICHELLE CASBON: I'm your
host, Michelle Casbon.

00:09:53.630 --> 00:09:56.155
I'm a developer programs
engineer here at Google Cloud.

00:09:56.155 --> 00:09:58.530
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And co-host
Mark Mirchandani, developer

00:09:58.530 --> 00:09:59.780
advocate here at Google Cloud.

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:02.550
MICHELLE CASBON: And we're very
excited about today's episode.

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:06.930
So John and I, we did a session
last week at Google Next.

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:09.120
And I was fascinated.

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:11.550
So meeting John and
preparing for our session,

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:13.668
I just learned so
many things about him.

00:10:13.668 --> 00:10:15.210
And I had to invite
him onto the show

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:17.310
to tell us more because
there are so many rabbit

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:17.670
holes to go down.

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:18.660
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And
you've been telling me

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:20.290
about this for this recording.

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:20.720
MICHELLE CASBON: That's right.

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:22.290
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So it's
super exciting for me

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:23.220
to kind of understand.

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:25.080
And we were talking
before the recording,

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:27.510
and I think John has a lot
of cool content to share.

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:29.247
But before we get
into that, John,

00:10:29.247 --> 00:10:31.080
can you tell us who you
are and what you do?

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:31.872
JOHN BOHANNON: Hey.

00:10:31.872 --> 00:10:34.110
So I'm John Bohannon,
and these days,

00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:36.510
I'm designing
artificial intelligence

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:39.038
to essentially read and write,
so that humans don't have to.

00:10:39.038 --> 00:10:40.830
MICHELLE CASBON: That
sounds very exciting.

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:43.980
Is this because humans are lazy
or because maybe their results

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:44.590
are better?

00:10:44.590 --> 00:10:45.930
JOHN BOHANNON: No,
humans work very hard.

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:47.513
And in fact, the
things we're building

00:10:47.513 --> 00:10:49.050
are most useful
for the humans who

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:51.900
work really hard, whose job
involves reading and writing.

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:54.880
So broadly speaking, if
your job is an analyst--

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:57.600
you could be in government,
or banking, or corporate,

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.460
or even a journalist,
which is something

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:00.793
that I've done in my life--

00:11:00.793 --> 00:11:02.460
it kind of boils down
to the same thing.

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:04.650
You spend the first
half of your day reading

00:11:04.650 --> 00:11:07.790
way more than you could possibly
read, second half writing,

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:10.380
make sense of what you read, and
help someone make a decision,

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:13.530
or inform the public, or
inform the decision maker.

00:11:13.530 --> 00:11:15.810
We try and automate
as much as possible

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:17.352
of that stack of tasks.

00:11:17.352 --> 00:11:18.810
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So what does that

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:22.230
mean when you say automate
something like reading?

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:23.110
How do you do that?

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:24.960
JOHN BOHANNON: So for
example, when I first

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:27.240
joined Primer, when
we were very small,

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:33.540
I was leading an effort to build
a product called Primer Science

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:35.430
that reads papers for you.

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.850
And so it was a really
weird situation,

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:40.350
kind of like a snake eating its
own tail, I soon discovered.

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:44.160
Because I found myself
using this thing in order

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:47.190
to more efficiently
find scientific papers

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:48.720
that describe the
latest techniques

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:51.930
that I needed to
build the thing.

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:54.180
So I was basically
making something

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:55.720
that would help me make it.

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:57.003
And we've come a long way.

00:11:57.003 --> 00:11:58.920
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well,
it's been a good chat.

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:00.180
Thanks for joining, everybody.

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:02.200
No, I mean, it's very confusing.

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:05.130
But the idea that you're
trying to write something

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:07.770
to help power itself is
kind of a scary concept,

00:12:07.770 --> 00:12:08.478
in a lot of ways.

00:12:08.478 --> 00:12:10.478
JOHN BOHANNON: I think
it's actually less scary.

00:12:10.478 --> 00:12:12.150
So this is not
singularity stuff.

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:14.790
This is honestly human
augmentation stuff.

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:17.070
Even in a dream scenario
where technologies

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:19.920
that augment humans go
way beyond what we think

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:21.880
is possible currently,
at the end of the day,

00:12:21.880 --> 00:12:24.540
this thing is not aimed towards
becoming a terminator robot.

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:26.610
[OMINOUS MUSIC]

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:29.610
It's aimed at making humans
that are much better at whatever

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:30.420
they care about.

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:33.150
MICHELLE CASBON: Right, so
give us an example of a way

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:34.775
that your system
has helped humans.

00:12:34.775 --> 00:12:36.150
How has it made
their jobs easier

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:37.410
or made them more productive?

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:39.700
JOHN BOHANNON: Yeah,
so for example,

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:43.620
we have a customer who is a
health insurance company, one

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:45.220
of the biggest in the world.

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:48.300
And they have hundreds
of medical policies.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:52.620
And there is a team of humans
whose job it is to constantly--

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:54.180
and I mean around the clock--

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:56.910
do scientific review to make
sure that those policies are up

00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:57.660
to date.

00:12:57.660 --> 00:12:59.288
And it's a legal
requirement, but also

00:12:59.288 --> 00:13:00.330
a functional requirement.

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:02.640
If you're going to have
policies that cover the latest

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:05.438
techniques and diseases
as we learn about them,

00:13:05.438 --> 00:13:07.480
someone has to actually
keep up with the science.

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:09.840
And that is an impossible task.

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:12.360
Even for that team,
which is a big team who

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:14.500
are really good
at this, there are

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:18.570
on order of at least 10 times
too many papers to read--

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:19.390
at least.

00:13:19.390 --> 00:13:21.150
So what they do is
they take shortcuts,

00:13:21.150 --> 00:13:22.500
like we all do as humans.

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:24.450
They rely on advice from people.

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:25.560
They read review papers.

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:26.730
Then, hopefully,
that review paper

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:28.320
kind of covered
enough that I don't

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:30.580
have to read all the
things that went into it.

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:32.087
And also just random sampling.

00:13:32.087 --> 00:13:34.170
I think that is actually
the main technique humans

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:35.910
use when there's too
much for them to do,

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:37.170
is they just kind
of random sample

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:39.550
and hope that they're more or
less covering whatever it is.

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:41.100
So anyway, we have
this system set up

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:45.240
to just read all PubMed
papers for them constantly.

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:48.750
And there are classifiers that
are working in the background

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.000
to help them find the
things that are actually

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:52.500
relevant to any given policy.

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:53.460
They still have to read papers.

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:55.085
They still have to
make sense of stuff.

00:13:55.085 --> 00:13:58.080
But they get a huge compression
of that information.

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:01.920
So everything is topic modeled,
and binned, and summarized.

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:06.300
And the most impactful papers
as described in the news or even

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:08.820
on Twitter are all being
kind of sequenced together

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:10.215
and compressed.

00:14:10.215 --> 00:14:12.690
MICHELLE CASBON: Nice, so
you're saving people time.

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:14.458
You're helping
them do their jobs.

00:14:14.458 --> 00:14:16.000
Where did you come
up with this idea?

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:17.978
Where did the idea for
this system come from?

00:14:17.978 --> 00:14:19.770
JOHN BOHANNON: Oh, I
mean, originally, this

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:21.450
was the first thing
built at Primer,

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:22.900
and it was just a prototype.

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:24.917
We know we're onto
something with automating

00:14:24.917 --> 00:14:25.750
reading and writing.

00:14:25.750 --> 00:14:27.630
If we can do it on a
really hard document

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:29.310
type-- a scientific paper.

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:30.727
So it was really
just a prototype.

00:14:30.727 --> 00:14:33.227
MARK MIRCHANDANI: It kind of
reminds me of the college days,

00:14:33.227 --> 00:14:35.610
when I mean, really, I'm sure
a lot of the college people

00:14:35.610 --> 00:14:37.020
have kind of come up
with these shortcuts

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:39.210
naturally because that's
how the human brain worked.

00:14:39.210 --> 00:14:41.502
Especially where-- I don't
know about y'all in college,

00:14:41.502 --> 00:14:43.620
but I was maybe not exactly
an exemplary student.

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:46.730
But the idea was you had a
tremendous amount of resources

00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:48.230
that you needed
to kind of consume

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:51.358
in order to usually turn out a
paper or something like that.

00:14:51.358 --> 00:14:52.650
And so there are all kinds of--

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:53.570
you get the short versions.

00:14:53.570 --> 00:14:54.510
You get these summaries.

00:14:54.510 --> 00:14:56.120
It sounds like that
is probably something

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:58.322
that is pretty instilled
into what humans naturally

00:14:58.322 --> 00:14:59.780
kind of tend to
do, especially when

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:02.527
you get more and more complex
things, like scientific papers.

00:15:02.527 --> 00:15:03.360
JOHN BOHANNON: Yeah.

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:05.030
And it's not just
the reading side.

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:07.902
So we also try and write the
first draft of things for you.

00:15:07.902 --> 00:15:09.860
The requirement here is
not to make it perfect,

00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:10.670
ready to publish.

00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:13.370
The goal is do better
than a blank page.

00:15:13.370 --> 00:15:15.820
Get something together that
you can work with quickly.

00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:17.570
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And
how is that process,

00:15:17.570 --> 00:15:20.210
going from blank page to what
I assume the first couple

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:22.790
renditions were maybe
not necessarily the most

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:24.200
readable of papers?

00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:26.600
JOHN BOHANNON: Oh, well, I
mean, the cool thing is just

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:29.540
over the past two years of
building technology at Primer,

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:32.067
I've covered the whole
arc of approaches.

00:15:32.067 --> 00:15:34.400
So in the beginning, you start
with rule based systems--

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:34.900
heuristics.

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:39.290
You just write exactly what will
be written with substitutions.

00:15:39.290 --> 00:15:41.870
If you get this as data,
then say it this way.

00:15:41.870 --> 00:15:42.470
Insert thing.

00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:45.137
It's like Madlibbing, but it can
get really, really complicated.

00:15:45.137 --> 00:15:48.260
And in fact, that's why
people ultimately turn away

00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:51.080
from these systems, is like
if you have a problem that

00:15:51.080 --> 00:15:54.237
is sufficiently complex,
no amount of rule writing

00:15:54.237 --> 00:15:55.320
is going to get you there.

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:56.490
It's just too brittle.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:58.407
You can't capture the
complexity of the world.

00:15:58.407 --> 00:16:01.550
Beyond that, what you can do
is use extractive techniques.

00:16:01.550 --> 00:16:03.830
So you have input
documents, and you

00:16:03.830 --> 00:16:06.740
have a system that finds the
bits of those documents that

00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:10.700
are most relevant to whatever
the need is, and then assembles

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:12.560
them together,
Frankenstein style.

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:13.947
And it mostly works.

00:16:13.947 --> 00:16:15.530
One thing that's
really nice is you're

00:16:15.530 --> 00:16:19.220
guaranteed that any given
sentence that the system says

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:21.500
will at least be a reasonable
sentence because it

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:22.522
was written by a human.

00:16:22.522 --> 00:16:24.980
What you're not guaranteed is
that the whole thing actually

00:16:24.980 --> 00:16:27.230
works at that paragraph
or document level.

00:16:27.230 --> 00:16:29.780
And then the dream
is abstractive,

00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:33.860
where you give the system
access to the English language,

00:16:33.860 --> 00:16:37.590
or even just the English
alphanumeric character system.

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:39.380
And it goes sequence
to sequence.

00:16:39.380 --> 00:16:40.700
Basically, things come in.

00:16:40.700 --> 00:16:42.140
It decides what
to say, basically

00:16:42.140 --> 00:16:45.210
word by word or character by
character, and spits out text.

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:47.915
The problem with that is it
runs off the rails very quickly

00:16:47.915 --> 00:16:48.950
and becomes incoherent.

00:16:48.950 --> 00:16:50.310
No one has solved that problem.

00:16:50.310 --> 00:16:51.980
In fact, people
right here at Google

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:54.230
have done some of the most
groundbreaking work in this

00:16:54.230 --> 00:16:55.438
and still haven't cracked it.

00:16:55.438 --> 00:16:58.400
So the big news that came
out a month or two ago

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:02.090
was that OpenAI had made a
universal language model, which

00:17:02.090 --> 00:17:04.700
is sort of the new
hotness in NLP, that

00:17:04.700 --> 00:17:08.240
was capable of generating
totally coherent text,

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:09.500
paragraphs at a time.

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:13.970
And it is true that often,
not always, it can do that.

00:17:13.970 --> 00:17:16.819
But it's basically
just like sleepwalking.

00:17:16.819 --> 00:17:18.319
You can't tell it what to say.

00:17:18.319 --> 00:17:20.810
It won't say anything
actually useful.

00:17:20.810 --> 00:17:23.950
But it will spit out
locally coherent text.

00:17:23.950 --> 00:17:25.700
That's where we are
with state of the art.

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:28.099
And there's actually
another chapter

00:17:28.099 --> 00:17:30.470
to this saga that's going
to be starting soon.

00:17:30.470 --> 00:17:32.600
And we're playing a
small part in that.

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:35.030
We've got a paper coming
out in "Archive" tomorrow,

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:38.600
which is we are going to be
introducing a new way to do

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:42.530
text generation that reframes
the problem as what's

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:43.970
called question answering.

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:46.940
So in that case, it's
kind of like blurring

00:17:46.940 --> 00:17:50.198
the line between the extractive
and abstractive techniques.

00:17:50.198 --> 00:17:51.740
So in extractive,
you're only allowed

00:17:51.740 --> 00:17:54.073
to grab what humans have said
and stick it in front, one

00:17:54.073 --> 00:17:54.980
after the other.

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:57.440
In abstractive, you
just read, and then you

00:17:57.440 --> 00:17:59.517
grab whatever you want
from a dictionary.

00:17:59.517 --> 00:18:01.100
We've blurred the
line between the two

00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:04.910
by reframing this as,
you're asking a question

00:18:04.910 --> 00:18:08.450
and then looking into the text
as a dictionary for the answer.

00:18:08.450 --> 00:18:11.780
So the whole text
becomes, essentially,

00:18:11.780 --> 00:18:14.680
the vocabulary on offer, which
can range from a single comma--

00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:17.180
you could grab that and say,
yep, that's what I want to say.

00:18:17.180 --> 00:18:19.250
Or you can grab
an entire sentence

00:18:19.250 --> 00:18:22.280
and say, oh, it says exactly
what I need to say right now.

00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:23.150
Boom.

00:18:23.150 --> 00:18:26.130
And so you have a more
limited expressivity.

00:18:26.130 --> 00:18:28.640
But we're finding
that, actually, it's

00:18:28.640 --> 00:18:31.260
starting to pass the Turing
test with text generation.

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:33.800
So the problem that we are
publishing about tomorrow

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:35.810
is on headline generation.

00:18:35.810 --> 00:18:38.300
You can imagine a headline as
the shortest summary possible

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:39.228
of a document.

00:18:39.228 --> 00:18:41.270
A news headline is
essentially trying to tell you

00:18:41.270 --> 00:18:42.800
what this article's about.

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:46.400
But if you get anything
wrong, it's totally useless.

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:48.350
You can get away with
a bunch of fudges

00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:50.683
if you have to generate
a paragraph long summary.

00:18:50.683 --> 00:18:51.600
It's still functional.

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:52.400
You know what the
document's about.

00:18:52.400 --> 00:18:53.850
But if you change one word--

00:18:53.850 --> 00:18:56.240
x bombs y versus y bombs x--

00:18:56.240 --> 00:18:57.830
they have totally
different meanings.

00:18:57.830 --> 00:19:00.500
And of course, if you replace
one those words with the word

00:19:00.500 --> 00:19:04.140
wagon, then it's also
just totally meaningless.

00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:06.200
So by making the
system concentrate

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:10.190
on the vocabulary of the text,
we just happily discovered

00:19:10.190 --> 00:19:12.090
that it does amazingly well.

00:19:12.090 --> 00:19:13.970
So humans, vast
majority of the time,

00:19:13.970 --> 00:19:15.680
can't tell which
headline is actually

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:17.480
machine or human-generated.

00:19:17.480 --> 00:19:19.640
And in the majority
of cases, they

00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:21.710
judge not knowing
which is which.

00:19:21.710 --> 00:19:23.240
We have them score
these headlines,

00:19:23.240 --> 00:19:25.970
and they, more
often than not, say

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:29.270
that the machine-generated
headline was as good or better

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:30.183
than the original.

00:19:30.183 --> 00:19:32.350
And by the way, this was
all built on Google's Bert,

00:19:32.350 --> 00:19:34.017
so thank you, Google
Research, for that.

00:19:34.017 --> 00:19:34.933
MICHELLE CASBON: Nice.

00:19:34.933 --> 00:19:37.040
Yeah, I hear a lot of
positive things about Bert.

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:39.050
And it's really
fascinating to see how

00:19:39.050 --> 00:19:40.500
you've made this progression.

00:19:40.500 --> 00:19:42.270
So I've seen the
Quiksilver codebase,

00:19:42.270 --> 00:19:44.890
and it's just fascinating
to see some of those rules

00:19:44.890 --> 00:19:45.682
that you generated.

00:19:45.682 --> 00:19:47.223
There are literal
functions that just

00:19:47.223 --> 00:19:48.530
generate very specific things.

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:50.050
But then that's
combined with, I can

00:19:50.050 --> 00:19:52.480
see how you've progressed
as you've been there

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:54.010
and come up with
these new ideas.

00:19:54.010 --> 00:19:56.350
So I'm really excited to
see how the research that's

00:19:56.350 --> 00:19:57.040
in your paper--

00:19:57.040 --> 00:19:58.820
how that's reflected
in the codebase.

00:19:58.820 --> 00:19:58.895
JOHN BOHANNON: Yeah.

00:19:58.895 --> 00:20:00.070
And also, I think
everyone's going to start

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:01.330
using this technique now.

00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:04.120
It'll find applications far
beyond what we're using it for.

00:20:04.120 --> 00:20:05.350
MICHELLE CASBON: By the time
this episode is published,

00:20:05.350 --> 00:20:07.517
we'll have a link for you,
and everyone can read it.

00:20:07.517 --> 00:20:10.150
And what's fascinating is that
it's not your typical sequence

00:20:10.150 --> 00:20:11.170
to sequence generator.

00:20:11.170 --> 00:20:13.640
It's this totally new way
of looking at the problem,

00:20:13.640 --> 00:20:16.200
reframing it, and
it's fascinating.

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:18.700
JOHN BOHANNON: And it's made
possible by these big beautiful

00:20:18.700 --> 00:20:20.260
universal language
models that Google

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:23.260
released that, from an AI
engineering point of view,

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:24.280
is just a dream.

00:20:24.280 --> 00:20:26.922
All the hard work has been done,
and you just pop off the head

00:20:26.922 --> 00:20:28.130
and put on a different thing.

00:20:28.130 --> 00:20:28.785
It's like LEGO.

00:20:28.785 --> 00:20:29.410
It's beautiful.

00:20:29.410 --> 00:20:32.680
So we literally just took a
standard question answer head

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:34.030
and stuck it on top of Bert.

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:37.300
And then of course, we had
to do the training and all

00:20:37.300 --> 00:20:38.543
that stuff, but it works.

00:20:38.543 --> 00:20:40.210
MICHELLE CASBON: The
other thing I think

00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:43.637
is fascinating about that
is having seen your code,

00:20:43.637 --> 00:20:45.220
I was very surprised
to learn that you

00:20:45.220 --> 00:20:47.680
don't have a classical
computer science background.

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:50.608
So how did you get into building
something like Quicksilver?

00:20:50.608 --> 00:20:52.150
JOHN BOHANNON: Long
and winding path.

00:20:52.150 --> 00:20:54.970
So I started off as a
molecular biologist,

00:20:54.970 --> 00:20:57.010
studying the
evolution of bacteria.

00:20:57.010 --> 00:20:59.530
And fun fact-- I
designed a pregnancy

00:20:59.530 --> 00:21:01.310
test for codfish that worked.

00:21:01.310 --> 00:21:03.520
But then my PhD was
on a soil bacterium

00:21:03.520 --> 00:21:06.430
and trying to reconstruct,
essentially, Darwinian laws

00:21:06.430 --> 00:21:08.000
using experimental evolution.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:08.500
And then--

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:10.180
MICHELLE CASBON: Wait, can we
go back to the pregnancy test?

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:11.290
What was that useful for?

00:21:11.290 --> 00:21:13.540
JOHN BOHANNON: Oh, well, the
National Marine Fisheries

00:21:13.540 --> 00:21:16.300
Service needs to survey
the cod population.

00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:18.110
And at the time,
what you had to do

00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:20.950
was pull up net loads
of fish, cut them open,

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:25.060
and weigh organs, and see
if the females were gravid--

00:21:25.060 --> 00:21:26.410
having lots of eggs.

00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:29.020
And not only is it really
inefficient as a sampling

00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:31.385
technique, but of course,
you can't throw the fish back

00:21:31.385 --> 00:21:32.510
after you've cut them open.

00:21:32.510 --> 00:21:34.305
And so that, among
other reasons,

00:21:34.305 --> 00:21:36.430
was motivation to get a
more sophisticated approach

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:37.878
to population surveys.

00:21:37.878 --> 00:21:40.420
MICHELLE CASBON: So you saved
cod lives by developing a test.

00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:41.295
JOHN BOHANNON: I did.

00:21:41.295 --> 00:21:44.205
I am responsible for
some number of cod lives.

00:21:44.205 --> 00:21:45.330
MICHELLE CASBON: Excellent.

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:46.420
OK, continue.

00:21:46.420 --> 00:21:48.367
JOHN BOHANNON: And
so, then, I wanted

00:21:48.367 --> 00:21:50.200
to do something different,
instead of diving

00:21:50.200 --> 00:21:52.390
into the next lab
job for my postdoc.

00:21:52.390 --> 00:21:57.160
And so I sent a letter to the
editor of "Science," saying,

00:21:57.160 --> 00:21:59.350
I hear that you
might have some kind

00:21:59.350 --> 00:22:01.780
of interesting opportunities
for someone like me.

00:22:01.780 --> 00:22:04.000
I'm a molecular biologist
and a playwright.

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:05.350
MICHELLE CASBON: Who has
saved all these cod lives.

00:22:05.350 --> 00:22:06.700
JOHN BOHANNON: No, I
didn't even bring that up.

00:22:06.700 --> 00:22:08.075
I think it was
literally as weird

00:22:08.075 --> 00:22:09.910
as, I'm a molecular
biologist playwright.

00:22:09.910 --> 00:22:10.390
Because I was--

00:22:10.390 --> 00:22:11.380
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well,
the playwright stuff,

00:22:11.380 --> 00:22:12.290
you haven't even
talked about yet.

00:22:12.290 --> 00:22:13.873
JOHN BOHANNON: Well,
so during my PhD,

00:22:13.873 --> 00:22:16.480
I was spending way too much of
my time doing extracurriculars,

00:22:16.480 --> 00:22:20.000
among which was a lot of
stagewriting and acting.

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:22.600
And what I later found out
was that my letter circulated

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:23.830
as a joke at "Science."

00:22:23.830 --> 00:22:25.330
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Well, I mean, it's

00:22:25.330 --> 00:22:26.830
good to be known
for something, but.

00:22:26.830 --> 00:22:29.163
JOHN BOHANNON: Well, that's
the beauty of this approach.

00:22:29.163 --> 00:22:32.110
So that was one of n spores
that I sent out on the winds,

00:22:32.110 --> 00:22:34.430
trying to find out what my
next life chapter should be.

00:22:34.430 --> 00:22:36.010
And so when I got a response,
I was kind of surprised

00:22:36.010 --> 00:22:38.830
and forgotten it, mostly,
that I had even sent that one.

00:22:38.830 --> 00:22:41.680
And I got a response
from the news editor

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:44.425
of "Science," who, in a
former life, was a novelist.

00:22:44.425 --> 00:22:46.300
He's now at the New York
Academy of Sciences.

00:22:46.300 --> 00:22:49.120
And he said, well, you have
an interesting background.

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:52.150
I think we have a
news internship.

00:22:52.150 --> 00:22:53.110
Would that be a fit?

00:22:53.110 --> 00:22:56.650
And I was in England
and doing my PhD.

00:22:56.650 --> 00:22:59.410
And not far away
at Cambridge, they

00:22:59.410 --> 00:23:01.510
had just set up the first
international office

00:23:01.510 --> 00:23:02.560
for "Science."

00:23:02.560 --> 00:23:04.990
And the news editor
there was just

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:07.600
setting up shop and kind of
wanted to have a news intern,

00:23:07.600 --> 00:23:08.960
but no one knew about it.

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:12.100
And so I just snuck
in, not a single piece

00:23:12.100 --> 00:23:13.060
of writing to my name.

00:23:13.060 --> 00:23:14.060
MICHELLE CASBON: Wow.

00:23:14.060 --> 00:23:14.920
What year was that?

00:23:14.920 --> 00:23:15.700
Because I was in
Cambridge as well.

00:23:15.700 --> 00:23:16.887
JOHN BOHANNON: 2002.

00:23:16.887 --> 00:23:18.970
MICHELLE CASBON: 2002, I
was not at Cambridge yet.

00:23:18.970 --> 00:23:22.127
So you started out as a news
intern in Cambridge in England.

00:23:22.127 --> 00:23:22.960
JOHN BOHANNON: Yeah.

00:23:22.960 --> 00:23:23.980
MARK MIRCHANDANI: With a PhD.

00:23:23.980 --> 00:23:24.772
JOHN BOHANNON: Yes.

00:23:24.772 --> 00:23:28.060
And within a couple
months of doing that,

00:23:28.060 --> 00:23:31.990
I found myself in an
obscure city in China

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:35.920
investigating a body factory
where human cadavers are

00:23:35.920 --> 00:23:36.880
turned into artwork.

00:23:36.880 --> 00:23:39.703
[DRAMATIC MUSIC]

00:23:39.703 --> 00:23:40.620
MICHELLE CASBON: What?

00:23:40.620 --> 00:23:43.320
JOHN BOHANNON: Yes, where
my mission was to get proof,

00:23:43.320 --> 00:23:45.150
if it existed, that
this body factory

00:23:45.150 --> 00:23:48.817
was sourcing its human cadavers
from the Chinese government.

00:23:48.817 --> 00:23:51.150
It is the number one executer
of prisoners in the world,

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:53.130
so it seems like a
great source of bodies.

00:23:53.130 --> 00:23:55.320
And I got confirmation
they were getting bodies

00:23:55.320 --> 00:23:56.515
from the Chinese state.

00:23:56.515 --> 00:23:57.390
MICHELLE CASBON: Wow.

00:23:57.390 --> 00:23:59.390
JOHN BOHANNON: So when
I had that experience.

00:23:59.390 --> 00:24:00.640
I basically never looked back.

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:02.890
I was like, oh, this is what
I'm supposed to be doing.

00:24:02.890 --> 00:24:05.280
And I then did that
for more than a decade.

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:08.485
MICHELLE CASBON: And you
investigated many more exciting

00:24:08.485 --> 00:24:08.985
topics.

00:24:08.985 --> 00:24:09.750
JOHN BOHANNON: Yes.

00:24:09.750 --> 00:24:10.890
MICHELLE CASBON: What
was your favorite topic

00:24:10.890 --> 00:24:12.390
that you ever covered?

00:24:12.390 --> 00:24:14.790
JOHN BOHANNON: It depends
on the flavor of favor.

00:24:14.790 --> 00:24:15.260
MICHELLE CASBON: OK, so--

00:24:15.260 --> 00:24:16.800
JOHN BOHANNON: It depends on
what you mean by favorite.

00:24:16.800 --> 00:24:18.780
MICHELLE CASBON: What was
the most interesting story

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:19.590
you wrote about?

00:24:19.590 --> 00:24:21.510
JOHN BOHANNON: So
interesting to the audience

00:24:21.510 --> 00:24:26.760
was probably some of the
weirder stories that I've done.

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:31.710
I organized and ran the
first scientific conference

00:24:31.710 --> 00:24:34.650
within a virtual game
world, "World of Warcraft,"

00:24:34.650 --> 00:24:37.570
with the director of
human-centered computing

00:24:37.570 --> 00:24:38.070
at the NSF.

00:24:38.070 --> 00:24:40.180
MARK MIRCHANDANI: The real
question is, what level was he?

00:24:40.180 --> 00:24:41.980
JOHN BOHANNON: 70,
which, at the time, was--

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:42.310
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Of course.

00:24:42.310 --> 00:24:42.960
That was the max then.

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:44.450
I just don't know
if it still is now.

00:24:44.450 --> 00:24:44.490
Maybe.

00:24:44.490 --> 00:24:45.090
JOHN BOHANNON: I don't either.

00:24:45.090 --> 00:24:47.580
MARK MIRCHANDANI: But I mean,
that actually is now not

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:48.770
incredibly uncommon, right?

00:24:48.770 --> 00:24:52.170
They've started to have a lot
of event-led meet-ups inside

00:24:52.170 --> 00:24:55.140
of virtual worlds, whether
that is popular video games

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:57.810
or whether they've had all this
"Fortnite" stuff and all those.

00:24:57.810 --> 00:24:59.220
I mean, it's been
tremendously interesting

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:01.015
to see the intersection
between real life

00:25:01.015 --> 00:25:03.390
and where people want to bring
their interests into that,

00:25:03.390 --> 00:25:04.650
even though they're not
related to the game.

00:25:04.650 --> 00:25:07.500
So maybe you kind of pioneered
a little bit of that effort?

00:25:07.500 --> 00:25:08.917
JOHN BOHANNON:
Yeah, I think I was

00:25:08.917 --> 00:25:10.367
part of that that first wave.

00:25:10.367 --> 00:25:12.450
And it does make things
possible that are just not

00:25:12.450 --> 00:25:13.480
practical in the real world.

00:25:13.480 --> 00:25:15.855
For example, for entertainment
during this big scientific

00:25:15.855 --> 00:25:17.640
conference, we
went out on a raid,

00:25:17.640 --> 00:25:23.070
and absolutely everyone got
killed in horrible ways.

00:25:23.070 --> 00:25:25.590
And then we got to laugh
about it over virtual beers.

00:25:25.590 --> 00:25:26.870
MARK MIRCHANDANI: The most
amazing thing I've heard.

00:25:26.870 --> 00:25:27.990
JOHN BOHANNON: Yeah,
it was pretty great.

00:25:27.990 --> 00:25:29.490
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
That's awesome.

00:25:29.490 --> 00:25:33.060
JOHN BOHANNON: But yeah, I think
interesting to me was embedding

00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:36.060
with NATO forces in
Kandahar during the surge

00:25:36.060 --> 00:25:37.000
in Afghanistan.

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:40.620
And I was there to try
and convince the military

00:25:40.620 --> 00:25:42.297
to release a data set.

00:25:42.297 --> 00:25:44.880
They didn't realize it, but that
was my actual secret mission.

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:45.990
And I succeeded.

00:25:45.990 --> 00:25:50.220
And so I engineered the first
ever honest public release

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:53.340
of civilian casualty data from
the United Nations and NATO

00:25:53.340 --> 00:25:54.060
forces.

00:25:54.060 --> 00:25:56.010
And I say honest
because they actually

00:25:56.010 --> 00:25:58.530
used to do that routinely
during the Vietnam War.

00:25:58.530 --> 00:26:01.170
The US military would actually
publish casualty numbers

00:26:01.170 --> 00:26:02.430
in the newspaper.

00:26:02.430 --> 00:26:05.700
This was during the period of
the war of attrition, the idea

00:26:05.700 --> 00:26:08.948
being that if we were killing
10 communists for every one US

00:26:08.948 --> 00:26:09.990
soldier, we were winning.

00:26:09.990 --> 00:26:12.810
And so it was actually
my stats professor.

00:26:12.810 --> 00:26:16.590
When he was a grad student, he
started analyzing those numbers

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:18.990
and proved that they
were being cooked.

00:26:18.990 --> 00:26:21.570
Because the distribution
of the final digit

00:26:21.570 --> 00:26:23.920
was not following a random
distribution at all.

00:26:23.920 --> 00:26:26.362
Can you guess which numbers
were abnormally infrequent?

00:26:26.362 --> 00:26:27.570
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Infrequent?

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:28.350
MICHELLE CASBON: 0.

00:26:28.350 --> 00:26:28.920
JOHN BOHANNON: You got it.

00:26:28.920 --> 00:26:30.150
If you're going to
publish fake numbers,

00:26:30.150 --> 00:26:31.800
you better make it
feel real by saying

00:26:31.800 --> 00:26:34.265
that 27 communists were killed.

00:26:34.265 --> 00:26:36.932
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So if you have
one takeaway from this episode,

00:26:36.932 --> 00:26:40.690
it's to make sure you correctly
falsify numbers by using

00:26:40.690 --> 00:26:41.940
an actual random distribution.

00:26:41.940 --> 00:26:42.900
JOHN BOHANNON: Exactly.

00:26:42.900 --> 00:26:44.100
If you're going to cook
the books, do it right.

00:26:44.100 --> 00:26:45.480
MICHELLE CASBON: So how were
you able to convince them

00:26:45.480 --> 00:26:47.277
to release this honest data?

00:26:47.277 --> 00:26:49.860
JOHN BOHANNON: So first of all,
it was just a lot of face time

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:53.340
with officers in Kandahar,
and visiting hospitals,

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:55.190
and just sort of being
there with the teams.

00:26:55.190 --> 00:26:55.690
So--

00:26:55.690 --> 00:26:57.050
MICHELLE CASBON: And you were
there as press as a journalist?

00:26:57.050 --> 00:26:57.967
JOHN BOHANNON: Mm-hmm.

00:26:57.967 --> 00:27:00.450
And so I learned a lot about
the US military-- well, NATO,

00:27:00.450 --> 00:27:04.360
as well, but I was mostly around
a lot of US military officers.

00:27:04.360 --> 00:27:06.880
But I really did not
realize until I spent a lot

00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:11.190
time with them how much it is
like a face-to-face culture.

00:27:11.190 --> 00:27:13.920
It's such a vast and
inefficient bureaucracy

00:27:13.920 --> 00:27:16.080
that think that everything
is just sort of-- we

00:27:16.080 --> 00:27:16.740
even have a term for it.

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:18.490
Oh, that was done with
military precision.

00:27:18.490 --> 00:27:20.550
And that does mean
something, but actually how

00:27:20.550 --> 00:27:24.490
things move in such a huge
organization is not precise.

00:27:24.490 --> 00:27:25.500
It's very efficient.

00:27:25.500 --> 00:27:27.000
And basically, when
things get done,

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:28.950
it's because people are
really invested in it,

00:27:28.950 --> 00:27:30.012
and they make it happen.

00:27:30.012 --> 00:27:32.220
And so I feel like a lot of
what I did in Afghanistan

00:27:32.220 --> 00:27:34.890
was just convincing the
right people that this

00:27:34.890 --> 00:27:36.648
is the kind of
thing you should do.

00:27:36.648 --> 00:27:38.690
This data is not going to
do any harm to release.

00:27:38.690 --> 00:27:39.732
It will do a lot of good.

00:27:39.732 --> 00:27:41.880
And so I basically
asked for a little bit,

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:42.838
and I got a little bit.

00:27:42.838 --> 00:27:44.338
And I was like, oh,
this is really--

00:27:44.338 --> 00:27:46.320
no one's ever seen this,
and this really helps.

00:27:46.320 --> 00:27:47.987
I make the case that
you were describing

00:27:47.987 --> 00:27:50.520
about how, for example, this
new approach to roadblocks

00:27:50.520 --> 00:27:52.477
has started to reduce
civilian casualties.

00:27:52.477 --> 00:27:54.060
If you give me a
little bit more data,

00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:56.700
I could probably show that has
made a difference in regions

00:27:56.700 --> 00:27:58.307
where you stood up
that new technique.

00:27:58.307 --> 00:27:59.390
Release a little bit more.

00:27:59.390 --> 00:28:01.140
I was like, oh, that's
really interesting.

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:03.302
You know what else?

00:28:03.302 --> 00:28:05.760
This other thing that you really
care about-- if you've got

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:06.660
the data to show it.

00:28:06.660 --> 00:28:09.060
And slowly but surely, I had
everything but some patches.

00:28:09.060 --> 00:28:10.950
And I was like, well, why not
just release the whole thing?

00:28:10.950 --> 00:28:12.430
And it just became a lot easier.

00:28:12.430 --> 00:28:14.440
But there's no way I could
have done that from a distance.

00:28:14.440 --> 00:28:15.330
Just no way at all.

00:28:15.330 --> 00:28:15.960
MICHELLE CASBON:
And what did you

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:18.000
do with that data set once you
released it, once you had it?

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:18.750
JOHN BOHANNON: Published
it in "Science,"

00:28:18.750 --> 00:28:19.710
released it publicly.

00:28:19.710 --> 00:28:20.870
MICHELLE CASBON: What did other
people do with that data set?

00:28:20.870 --> 00:28:21.370
Do you know?

00:28:21.370 --> 00:28:23.940
JOHN BOHANNON: A lot of
analysis, a lot of advocating.

00:28:23.940 --> 00:28:26.010
There was a more
than two-fold gap

00:28:26.010 --> 00:28:29.400
in the number of casualties
reported by the military

00:28:29.400 --> 00:28:30.840
compared to the UN observers.

00:28:30.840 --> 00:28:32.550
And we could see that
for the first time,

00:28:32.550 --> 00:28:34.480
and that had to
be accounted for.

00:28:34.480 --> 00:28:36.588
So it just basically
turned on the discourse.

00:28:36.588 --> 00:28:38.505
Everyone suddenly had
something to talk about.

00:28:38.505 --> 00:28:42.030
I think it just planted
a flag in history

00:28:42.030 --> 00:28:44.340
that if you are going to
be a huge occupying force,

00:28:44.340 --> 00:28:46.500
for better or worse, you
better be transparent.

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:50.070
But we now have conflicts in
Syria, where there is no one,

00:28:50.070 --> 00:28:51.630
essentially, to
hold accountable.

00:28:51.630 --> 00:28:53.945
So it's not a fix for
our bigger problems.

00:28:53.945 --> 00:28:55.320
But it was important
at the time.

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:57.270
MICHELLE CASBON: OK, so at this
point, you're a journalist.

00:28:57.270 --> 00:28:59.312
You've been traveling
around the world, following

00:28:59.312 --> 00:29:00.790
these really exciting stories.

00:29:00.790 --> 00:29:04.683
So how do you get from that
kind of lifestyle to AI?

00:29:04.683 --> 00:29:05.600
JOHN BOHANNON: Python.

00:29:05.600 --> 00:29:06.480
MICHELLE CASBON: Python.

00:29:06.480 --> 00:29:07.935
JOHN BOHANNON: Of
course it was Python.

00:29:07.935 --> 00:29:09.970
MICHELLE CASBON: Like the snake,
because you're a biologist?

00:29:09.970 --> 00:29:10.530
JOHN BOHANNON: No, the--

00:29:10.530 --> 00:29:11.550
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Wow, good callback.

00:29:11.550 --> 00:29:12.720
JOHN BOHANNON: Pretty good.

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:14.490
No, the coding language.

00:29:14.490 --> 00:29:18.030
I had a project involving
the Google Ngrams data set.

00:29:18.030 --> 00:29:21.210
So the team at Harvard that
built the data set, as well

00:29:21.210 --> 00:29:23.370
as the Ngram Viewer, which
is alive and well today

00:29:23.370 --> 00:29:25.830
and really cool, it grew out
of the Google Books project.

00:29:25.830 --> 00:29:28.347
Those two people, grad
students at Harvard,

00:29:28.347 --> 00:29:29.430
were just friends of mine.

00:29:29.430 --> 00:29:31.050
I was basically
like moonlighting

00:29:31.050 --> 00:29:32.740
as a researcher in that group.

00:29:32.740 --> 00:29:35.310
And so when that paper came
out on the cover of "Science,"

00:29:35.310 --> 00:29:37.290
along with it was
a story that I did

00:29:37.290 --> 00:29:40.380
about the history
of science as seen

00:29:40.380 --> 00:29:41.970
through the lens
of that data set.

00:29:41.970 --> 00:29:44.580
And it was a really
frustrating experience for me

00:29:44.580 --> 00:29:48.510
because I was beholden to
this undergrad at Harvard who

00:29:48.510 --> 00:29:51.623
had to do the very basic
data manipulations for me.

00:29:51.623 --> 00:29:53.040
Because the data
sets were far too

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:54.630
large to deal with
in spreadsheets,

00:29:54.630 --> 00:29:56.070
and I didn't know
how to code yet.

00:29:56.070 --> 00:29:58.800
And I knew exactly
what I wanted to do.

00:29:58.800 --> 00:30:00.420
I could write it
down, like do this.

00:30:00.420 --> 00:30:02.260
But I couldn't
express it in code.

00:30:02.260 --> 00:30:04.710
And so the day after
that got published,

00:30:04.710 --> 00:30:06.157
I was like, enough is enough.

00:30:06.157 --> 00:30:08.490
I've gotta learn how to solve
these problems for myself.

00:30:08.490 --> 00:30:09.893
And well, I got obsessed.

00:30:09.893 --> 00:30:11.310
Once you have a
hammer, everything

00:30:11.310 --> 00:30:12.460
looks like a coding problem.

00:30:12.460 --> 00:30:12.900
So--

00:30:12.900 --> 00:30:13.770
MICHELLE CASBON: I don't know
what you're talking about.

00:30:13.770 --> 00:30:16.410
Using Python to solve every
single problem you encounter?

00:30:16.410 --> 00:30:18.327
No one in this room has
ever done that before.

00:30:18.327 --> 00:30:20.670
MARK MIRCHANDANI: That wouldn't
be a realistic scenario.

00:30:20.670 --> 00:30:22.110
JOHN BOHANNON: So I got
obsessed about coding.

00:30:22.110 --> 00:30:23.700
And then slowly
but surely, I went

00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:26.562
from being a foreign
correspondent who runs around

00:30:26.562 --> 00:30:28.770
to places like China and
Afghanistan to find stories,

00:30:28.770 --> 00:30:31.020
to someone who tries
to get data sets

00:30:31.020 --> 00:30:33.450
and pull out stories
worth telling from data.

00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:36.510
And then it became like
building things that read data.

00:30:36.510 --> 00:30:38.400
And then it became
like, wow, maybe

00:30:38.400 --> 00:30:40.855
I could actually contribute
to open source coding

00:30:40.855 --> 00:30:42.480
and then share some
of these solutions.

00:30:42.480 --> 00:30:46.210
And then I moved to the Bay Area
to do a visiting postdoc thing

00:30:46.210 --> 00:30:48.210
at Berkeley in their new
Data Science Institute.

00:30:48.210 --> 00:30:51.480
And my friend Sean Gourley,
just across the water

00:30:51.480 --> 00:30:53.888
in San Francisco, had a
new startup called Primer.

00:30:53.888 --> 00:30:55.680
And he'd tried this
trick on me before when

00:30:55.680 --> 00:30:57.167
he had a startup before this.

00:30:57.167 --> 00:30:58.125
He was like, just come.

00:30:58.125 --> 00:31:00.990
Come over, and we have really
cool resources and data.

00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:02.400
You should just come play.

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:04.350
And now that I lived in the
same city, I was like, ugh.

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:05.683
All right, all right, all right.

00:31:05.683 --> 00:31:08.352
And after like one
day at Primer--

00:31:08.352 --> 00:31:10.560
well, there were only 10
people, so it was a big room

00:31:10.560 --> 00:31:11.430
with a bunch of empty desks.

00:31:11.430 --> 00:31:13.263
And I'm sitting there
working on the problem

00:31:13.263 --> 00:31:17.160
at the time, which was I wanted
to make a quote extractor.

00:31:17.160 --> 00:31:21.120
So this was 2015,
and I wanted to get

00:31:21.120 --> 00:31:22.530
all the quotes of Donald Trump.

00:31:22.530 --> 00:31:24.780
My journalist colleagues
were chasing their own tails,

00:31:24.780 --> 00:31:27.780
trying to catch him
contradicting himself

00:31:27.780 --> 00:31:32.010
in any of his copious
publicly quoted statements.

00:31:32.010 --> 00:31:35.040
And I was surprised to learn
there is no database of quotes

00:31:35.040 --> 00:31:37.710
from him, nor is there a
database of quotes from anyone.

00:31:37.710 --> 00:31:39.710
And so I started to look
into it, got on Github,

00:31:39.710 --> 00:31:42.510
found that someone had made a
good first attempt at a quote

00:31:42.510 --> 00:31:43.170
extractor.

00:31:43.170 --> 00:31:45.150
And then the project
kind of petered out.

00:31:45.150 --> 00:31:48.030
And I actually tracked down
that person, and I was like,

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:49.110
what happened to this?

00:31:49.110 --> 00:31:50.460
I'd love to take this further.

00:31:50.460 --> 00:31:51.370
Why did you stop?

00:31:51.370 --> 00:31:53.400
And he was like,
because it's hard,

00:31:53.400 --> 00:31:55.063
and I need to get
paid for something.

00:31:55.063 --> 00:31:56.980
MARK MIRCHANDANI: What
about it makes it hard?

00:31:56.980 --> 00:31:58.500
JOHN BOHANNON: So you'd
think that you could just

00:31:58.500 --> 00:32:00.330
make a regular expression
that would find anything

00:32:00.330 --> 00:32:01.510
between quotation marks.

00:32:01.510 --> 00:32:01.770
But--

00:32:01.770 --> 00:32:02.880
MICHELLE CASBON:
Famous last words.

00:32:02.880 --> 00:32:03.660
JOHN BOHANNON: Well,
you're also going

00:32:03.660 --> 00:32:06.360
to capture anything
that's in quotation marks,

00:32:06.360 --> 00:32:07.190
like scare quotes.

00:32:07.190 --> 00:32:08.940
Here's the review of
the movie, "Titanic,"

00:32:08.940 --> 00:32:10.330
where "Titanic" is in quotes.

00:32:10.330 --> 00:32:12.720
Now you've essentially
got a huge source of junk

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:13.770
in things that are
supposed to be quotes.

00:32:13.770 --> 00:32:15.130
Then you've got an
even harder problem.

00:32:15.130 --> 00:32:16.860
Let's say you did
have clean quotes.

00:32:16.860 --> 00:32:20.190
You've just got the perfect
data set of truly quoted

00:32:20.190 --> 00:32:21.150
human statements.

00:32:21.150 --> 00:32:22.780
How do you figure
out who said it?

00:32:22.780 --> 00:32:24.373
So that's the problem
of attribution.

00:32:24.373 --> 00:32:26.790
So you could imagine making a
regular expression that will

00:32:26.790 --> 00:32:30.330
capture human names downstream--
you know, "said so-and-so"

00:32:30.330 --> 00:32:31.630
or "so-and-so says."

00:32:31.630 --> 00:32:33.870
And you might be able
to capture those spans,

00:32:33.870 --> 00:32:36.480
but it's a lot trickier
than you think.

00:32:36.480 --> 00:32:40.050
People basically have
a near infinite way

00:32:40.050 --> 00:32:42.690
of expressing that
someone said something

00:32:42.690 --> 00:32:44.490
and attributing it to them.

00:32:44.490 --> 00:32:47.280
And that didn't even touch
the he said, she said.

00:32:47.280 --> 00:32:49.860
Now you have a problem that's
a really classic hard problem

00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:53.430
in NLP called entity
co-reference resolution, where,

00:32:53.430 --> 00:32:56.070
given a text that mentions
multiple people that

00:32:56.070 --> 00:32:59.700
uses anaphora, like he and
she, him and her, his and hers,

00:32:59.700 --> 00:33:01.625
you have to figure
out who corresponds,

00:33:01.625 --> 00:33:03.000
which entity
actually corresponds

00:33:03.000 --> 00:33:04.770
to what bit of text.

00:33:04.770 --> 00:33:07.088
Now you're in
uncharted territory.

00:33:07.088 --> 00:33:08.130
So it's just really hard.

00:33:08.130 --> 00:33:11.640
So day one at Primer, I sat next
to this crazy Ukrainian named

00:33:11.640 --> 00:33:13.530
Leonard that Michelle has met.

00:33:13.530 --> 00:33:14.962
He's an absolute dear, and--

00:33:14.962 --> 00:33:16.920
MICHELLE CASBON: He is
hilarious and wonderful.

00:33:16.920 --> 00:33:17.610
JOHN BOHANNON: Yeah,
he's really great.

00:33:17.610 --> 00:33:18.900
MICHELLE CASBON: And
very skilled at NLP.

00:33:18.900 --> 00:33:20.608
JOHN BOHANNON: Yes,
he is, as we call it,

00:33:20.608 --> 00:33:21.840
a mensch worth mentioning.

00:33:21.840 --> 00:33:23.310
MICHELLE CASBON: Yes.

00:33:23.310 --> 00:33:24.060
Great description.

00:33:24.060 --> 00:33:25.650
JOHN BOHANNON: On
day one, [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:25.650 --> 00:33:26.650
what are you working on?

00:33:26.650 --> 00:33:28.600
And I was like digging
into this quote stuff.

00:33:28.600 --> 00:33:31.590
And he's like, oh, yeah,
we already solved that.

00:33:31.590 --> 00:33:33.870
The harder problem is this one.

00:33:33.870 --> 00:33:36.000
And he tries to show me
what he was working on.

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:39.178
And by the end of the day,
I was like, OK, I'll join.

00:33:39.178 --> 00:33:40.720
MICHELLE CASBON:
These are my people.

00:33:40.720 --> 00:33:41.650
JOHN BOHANNON: OK, fine.

00:33:41.650 --> 00:33:43.400
I could probably spend
the next six months

00:33:43.400 --> 00:33:45.580
of my life doing unpaid
work to try and fix quote

00:33:45.580 --> 00:33:46.990
extraction for everyone else.

00:33:46.990 --> 00:33:49.292
Or I could join a
startup that's trying

00:33:49.292 --> 00:33:51.500
to pull all that together
into true automated reading

00:33:51.500 --> 00:33:52.032
and writing.

00:33:52.032 --> 00:33:54.490
MICHELLE CASBON: And I mean,
how can you say no to Leonard?

00:33:54.490 --> 00:33:55.030
JOHN BOHANNON: He's a dear.

00:33:55.030 --> 00:33:56.405
MICHELLE CASBON:
He is wonderful.

00:33:56.405 --> 00:33:58.480
OK, so your common
thread up to this point

00:33:58.480 --> 00:34:01.090
has been interesting
problems to solve,

00:34:01.090 --> 00:34:04.695
data, and finding sort of
new ways of doing things

00:34:04.695 --> 00:34:07.660
that other people haven't
seen before-- haven't applied

00:34:07.660 --> 00:34:08.870
to those types of problems.

00:34:08.870 --> 00:34:09.190
JOHN BOHANNON: Yeah.

00:34:09.190 --> 00:34:10.940
MICHELLE CASBON: OK,
so how does dance tie

00:34:10.940 --> 00:34:12.070
in to your interest in AI?

00:34:12.070 --> 00:34:13.510
JOHN BOHANNON: Oh, yeah.

00:34:13.510 --> 00:34:15.060
That's a weird
cul-de-sac in my life.

00:34:15.060 --> 00:34:16.330
MICHELLE CASBON: Well, you
were already a playwright.

00:34:16.330 --> 00:34:18.040
You're already used
to being onstage.

00:34:18.040 --> 00:34:19.780
JOHN BOHANNON: So
OK, I was living

00:34:19.780 --> 00:34:25.690
in Vienna, Austria as a foreign
correspondent in 2006 and 2007

00:34:25.690 --> 00:34:28.317
when I had this party that
I was helping organize.

00:34:28.317 --> 00:34:30.400
It was a New Year's Eve
party that didn't actually

00:34:30.400 --> 00:34:31.810
happen on New Year's
Eve, but whatever.

00:34:31.810 --> 00:34:33.227
It was round about
New Year's Eve.

00:34:33.227 --> 00:34:35.590
And I wanted it to
be a dance party.

00:34:35.590 --> 00:34:38.409
I just always feel disappointed
at a nice big party

00:34:38.409 --> 00:34:39.610
when no dancing occurs.

00:34:39.610 --> 00:34:41.960
I just feel like it's a
crime against humanity.

00:34:41.960 --> 00:34:43.389
And so-- that's too strong.

00:34:43.389 --> 00:34:44.949
MARK MIRCHANDANI: We've heard
about some different stories

00:34:44.949 --> 00:34:45.449
today.

00:34:45.449 --> 00:34:46.992
I feel like the
dance party might not

00:34:46.992 --> 00:34:48.701
be at the top of the list.

00:34:48.701 --> 00:34:51.159
JOHN BOHANNON: It's hard to
get people to dance in general.

00:34:51.159 --> 00:34:52.617
And it's actually
surprisingly hard

00:34:52.617 --> 00:34:55.330
to get a group of frustrated
scientists, grad students

00:34:55.330 --> 00:34:56.598
to dance.

00:34:56.598 --> 00:34:57.640
I don't know why that is.

00:34:57.640 --> 00:34:59.280
It's just like, it's
a pretty tough crowd

00:34:59.280 --> 00:34:59.400
to get to loosen up.

00:34:59.400 --> 00:35:01.268
MICHELLE CASBON: I know
exactly why that is.

00:35:01.268 --> 00:35:03.310
JOHN BOHANNON: Yeah, but
is it nature or nurture?

00:35:03.310 --> 00:35:05.800
Are people who don't want to
dance becoming grad students?

00:35:05.800 --> 00:35:08.980
Or is there something about
the grad life experience that

00:35:08.980 --> 00:35:10.390
makes you not want to dance?

00:35:10.390 --> 00:35:11.315
Unsolved problems.

00:35:11.315 --> 00:35:11.815
So--

00:35:11.815 --> 00:35:12.460
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
I'm sure there's

00:35:12.460 --> 00:35:14.800
a machine learning algorithm
out there that could probably

00:35:14.800 --> 00:35:15.610
figure this out for us.

00:35:15.610 --> 00:35:16.540
JOHN BOHANNON: For all
you listening at home,

00:35:16.540 --> 00:35:18.160
if you've got an
idea about this,

00:35:18.160 --> 00:35:22.160
post it on Twitter
at #ReasonsForDance.

00:35:22.160 --> 00:35:22.660
So--

00:35:22.660 --> 00:35:24.743
MARK MIRCHANDANI: The
reasons for dance movement--

00:35:24.743 --> 00:35:26.780
I could get into this.

00:35:26.780 --> 00:35:29.890
JOHN BOHANNON: So I
proposed a dance contest

00:35:29.890 --> 00:35:31.723
to motivate the
audience that were

00:35:31.723 --> 00:35:32.890
all scientist grad students.

00:35:32.890 --> 00:35:35.560
So I was like, you
have to dance your PhD.

00:35:35.560 --> 00:35:39.640
So in a short dance, you
must express the basic idea,

00:35:39.640 --> 00:35:44.080
the essence of your PhD, but
with no words, no jargon,

00:35:44.080 --> 00:35:45.070
no PowerPoint slides.

00:35:45.070 --> 00:35:46.420
You have to just dance it.

00:35:46.420 --> 00:35:48.045
MICHELLE CASBON:
Which honestly, to me,

00:35:48.045 --> 00:35:49.450
sounds harder than doing a PhD.

00:35:49.450 --> 00:35:50.830
JOHN BOHANNON: Well,
there are some people

00:35:50.830 --> 00:35:51.940
that feel the opposite.

00:35:51.940 --> 00:35:53.860
And this contest
is basically-- it's

00:35:53.860 --> 00:35:57.340
become a huge filter on the
world to find that population.

00:35:57.340 --> 00:36:00.640
We almost do-- we being me
and "Science," the journal,

00:36:00.640 --> 00:36:02.260
who hosts this
contest every year,

00:36:02.260 --> 00:36:04.060
for 11 years and running--

00:36:04.060 --> 00:36:05.460
we do minimal marketing.

00:36:05.460 --> 00:36:08.720
Really, it's just like
a post on Facebook.

00:36:08.720 --> 00:36:10.570
And we put out a
little story online.

00:36:10.570 --> 00:36:11.740
People just come
out of the woodwork

00:36:11.740 --> 00:36:14.198
from all over the world, from
countries all over the world,

00:36:14.198 --> 00:36:15.147
and take part.

00:36:15.147 --> 00:36:17.230
But the reason it turned
into a contest at all was

00:36:17.230 --> 00:36:20.080
because after that party,
so long ago in Vienna,

00:36:20.080 --> 00:36:22.960
we shot some good video of the
actual contest-- the dances--

00:36:22.960 --> 00:36:24.498
and put it online.

00:36:24.498 --> 00:36:26.290
And I wrote a story
for "Science" about it,

00:36:26.290 --> 00:36:27.160
just for fun.

00:36:27.160 --> 00:36:31.090
And then the emails started
coming in from scientists just

00:36:31.090 --> 00:36:33.497
all over the world,
saying, I'm ready.

00:36:33.497 --> 00:36:35.080
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
People were excited.

00:36:35.080 --> 00:36:36.760
JOHN BOHANNON: When does
next year's contest kick off?

00:36:36.760 --> 00:36:37.730
MICHELLE CASBON:
You filled a gap.

00:36:37.730 --> 00:36:38.500
There was a need.

00:36:38.500 --> 00:36:39.040
JOHN BOHANNON: There was.

00:36:39.040 --> 00:36:39.470
And I had no plans.

00:36:39.470 --> 00:36:40.360
MARK MIRCHANDANI: No
one knew it existed.

00:36:40.360 --> 00:36:41.735
JOHN BOHANNON: No
plans for this.

00:36:41.735 --> 00:36:44.308
But then I was like, I
guess we do have to do it.

00:36:44.308 --> 00:36:46.600
And because people were asking
from all over the world,

00:36:46.600 --> 00:36:47.830
I had no money for this.

00:36:47.830 --> 00:36:49.025
Can't do a live event.

00:36:49.025 --> 00:36:50.650
So I was like, oh,
well, we could do it

00:36:50.650 --> 00:36:51.790
as an online video contest.

00:36:51.790 --> 00:36:54.840
And YouTube around
about 2007, 2008,

00:36:54.840 --> 00:36:56.680
it was working pretty well.

00:36:56.680 --> 00:36:58.960
So I was like, OK, put
your dances on YouTube,

00:36:58.960 --> 00:37:01.335
send me the link, and I'll
assemble a judge of experts,

00:37:01.335 --> 00:37:02.710
like I did the
first time around,

00:37:02.710 --> 00:37:04.252
from the worlds of
science and dance.

00:37:04.252 --> 00:37:06.490
And they'll determine
the winners.

00:37:06.490 --> 00:37:09.730
And the prize when we started
was a one year subscription

00:37:09.730 --> 00:37:10.480
to "Science."

00:37:10.480 --> 00:37:12.310
MICHELLE CASBON: That's perfect.

00:37:12.310 --> 00:37:14.980
JOHN BOHANNON: And it hasn't
gotten much grander than that.

00:37:14.980 --> 00:37:18.225
It's like, you get 1,000 bucks
is as fancy as we've gotten.

00:37:18.225 --> 00:37:20.350
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well, I
think it's because people

00:37:20.350 --> 00:37:21.820
aren't doing it for the prize.

00:37:21.820 --> 00:37:23.200
They're doing it for
the passion of it.

00:37:23.200 --> 00:37:23.880
JOHN BOHANNON: Yeah.

00:37:23.880 --> 00:37:24.280
Honestly--

00:37:24.280 --> 00:37:24.730
MICHELLE CASBON: They
just need an excuse.

00:37:24.730 --> 00:37:25.060
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Which is exciting.

00:37:25.060 --> 00:37:26.200
JOHN BOHANNON:
--that is exactly it.

00:37:26.200 --> 00:37:27.825
They would have been
doing this anyway.

00:37:27.825 --> 00:37:30.832
They probably already were
doing it in the shower, and--

00:37:30.832 --> 00:37:32.540
MICHELLE CASBON: They
needed an audience.

00:37:32.540 --> 00:37:34.415
JOHN BOHANNON: They just
needed the audience.

00:37:34.415 --> 00:37:35.560
That's what this provides.

00:37:35.560 --> 00:37:37.270
MICHELLE CASBON: OK,
so all of a sudden,

00:37:37.270 --> 00:37:40.000
you have all these people
around the world describing

00:37:40.000 --> 00:37:41.557
scientific topics
through dance--

00:37:41.557 --> 00:37:42.390
JOHN BOHANNON: Yeah.

00:37:42.390 --> 00:37:42.410
MICHELLE CASBON: --and
expressing themselves.

00:37:42.410 --> 00:37:45.730
JOHN BOHANNON: Oh, by the
way, this past year's winner

00:37:45.730 --> 00:37:51.010
was a PhD student in
Canada, who studies quantum

00:37:51.010 --> 00:37:53.320
properties of materials.

00:37:53.320 --> 00:37:57.220
And he not only organized
this awesome dance video,

00:37:57.220 --> 00:38:01.960
but he wrote and performed
a song for that video.

00:38:01.960 --> 00:38:03.043
It's like so over the top.

00:38:03.043 --> 00:38:04.502
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
I mean, if you're

00:38:04.502 --> 00:38:06.040
going to go any
portion of the way,

00:38:06.040 --> 00:38:07.780
you may as well go
all the way and get

00:38:07.780 --> 00:38:09.370
your own original
composition as well.

00:38:09.370 --> 00:38:09.955
JOHN BOHANNON: Yeah.

00:38:09.955 --> 00:38:10.270
It's pretty amazing.

00:38:10.270 --> 00:38:11.710
MICHELLE CASBON: I mean, he was
already doing it in the shower.

00:38:11.710 --> 00:38:12.025
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Exactly.

00:38:12.025 --> 00:38:12.340
MICHELLE CASBON: So he
might as well record.

00:38:12.340 --> 00:38:13.500
JOHN BOHANNON: It's
pretty amazing.

00:38:13.500 --> 00:38:14.917
MICHELLE CASBON:
OK, so people are

00:38:14.917 --> 00:38:16.900
describing scientific
topics through dance.

00:38:16.900 --> 00:38:21.590
Of course, that's a natural leap
through applying AI to that.

00:38:21.590 --> 00:38:22.840
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah, is it?

00:38:22.840 --> 00:38:24.130
Is it, though?

00:38:24.130 --> 00:38:26.995
Could you maybe clarify
that point a little bit?

00:38:26.995 --> 00:38:28.620
JOHN BOHANNON: How
do you mean exactly?

00:38:28.620 --> 00:38:30.040
MICHELLE CASBON: So I'm
trying to make the connection

00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:31.780
between the workshop I went to.

00:38:31.780 --> 00:38:33.590
There was this AI
and dance workshop.

00:38:33.590 --> 00:38:37.180
There's this group here in San
Francisco that meets weekly.

00:38:37.180 --> 00:38:38.370
And it's amazing.

00:38:38.370 --> 00:38:42.770
And I was baffled at how
AI and dance came together.

00:38:42.770 --> 00:38:45.310
And so I'm curious to hear
what the bridge was for you.

00:38:45.310 --> 00:38:49.863
How did you get from
dancer PhD to AI and dance?

00:38:49.863 --> 00:38:51.280
JOHN BOHANNON: So
yeah, that thing

00:38:51.280 --> 00:38:53.500
that happens every Wednesday
is called Kinetech Arts.

00:38:53.500 --> 00:38:55.000
And by the way,
it's a public event.

00:38:55.000 --> 00:38:56.890
So all you Bay Area
people who are listening,

00:38:56.890 --> 00:38:58.330
you are very welcome to join.

00:38:58.330 --> 00:38:59.580
You can find it on meetup.com.

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:04.360
And it's essentially a bunch of
technologists who get together

00:39:04.360 --> 00:39:07.420
with a bunch of mostly
dancers, but art people,

00:39:07.420 --> 00:39:09.200
and we do experiments together.

00:39:09.200 --> 00:39:12.550
So we'll wire up dancers
with motion capture suits

00:39:12.550 --> 00:39:15.400
and then run an experiment
where we generate music

00:39:15.400 --> 00:39:16.540
based on their movement.

00:39:16.540 --> 00:39:19.210
For example, one time we
brought in the research group

00:39:19.210 --> 00:39:24.010
from Berkeley who had that paper
out last year, in which you

00:39:24.010 --> 00:39:27.880
learn how to dance on
a human skeleton model

00:39:27.880 --> 00:39:31.780
by having the system just like
watch video of someone dancing

00:39:31.780 --> 00:39:35.450
and then apply that movement to
a model of someone else's body.

00:39:35.450 --> 00:39:39.370
So if you, Michelle, were to
take part, what would happen

00:39:39.370 --> 00:39:41.080
is you would just
be walking around,

00:39:41.080 --> 00:39:44.455
manipulating your limbs for a
while in a pretty random way,

00:39:44.455 --> 00:39:46.330
so that it gets sort of
all the angles of you

00:39:46.330 --> 00:39:48.460
and makes a model
of your skeleton.

00:39:48.460 --> 00:39:52.420
And then it can apply any
dance it's ever learned to you

00:39:52.420 --> 00:39:55.330
and generate a video of
you doing that dance.

00:39:55.330 --> 00:39:59.260
So I mean, you can make videos
of people dancing ballet

00:39:59.260 --> 00:40:01.720
at the level of the
Bolshoi, break dancing.

00:40:01.720 --> 00:40:03.220
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
The important part

00:40:03.220 --> 00:40:05.475
is that's not deeply terrifying.

00:40:05.475 --> 00:40:06.850
Because now we're
kind of getting

00:40:06.850 --> 00:40:09.040
into that-- what is it from
the past couple of months

00:40:09.040 --> 00:40:09.370
slash year?

00:40:09.370 --> 00:40:09.460
JOHN BOHANNON: Deepfakes.

00:40:09.460 --> 00:40:11.080
MARK MIRCHANDANI: It's this
whole deepfake concept, right?

00:40:11.080 --> 00:40:12.730
This idea that you
can now basically

00:40:12.730 --> 00:40:17.170
say, oh, video footage
is no longer reliable

00:40:17.170 --> 00:40:19.330
because it's too
easy to simulate.

00:40:19.330 --> 00:40:21.130
The master level that
we have now, you've

00:40:21.130 --> 00:40:23.590
got all these intense AI
and ML properties that

00:40:23.590 --> 00:40:25.390
are being put together
to say, anyone

00:40:25.390 --> 00:40:27.393
can be caught doing
anything or saying anything

00:40:27.393 --> 00:40:28.810
because we have
all the technology

00:40:28.810 --> 00:40:29.740
required to simulate it.

00:40:29.740 --> 00:40:30.532
JOHN BOHANNON: Yep.

00:40:30.532 --> 00:40:35.260
And while applying dance
movement to someone's body,

00:40:35.260 --> 00:40:37.890
it's hard to imagine how that
could go terribly wrong--

00:40:37.890 --> 00:40:40.770
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Oh, I have
many, many instant ideas

00:40:40.770 --> 00:40:42.020
of how that could go terribly.

00:40:42.020 --> 00:40:42.250
But please.

00:40:42.250 --> 00:40:44.710
JOHN BOHANNON: But I mean,
it's only a few steps away

00:40:44.710 --> 00:40:46.520
from a lot of terrible things.

00:40:46.520 --> 00:40:49.180
Yeah, I wish I could say
that we have enough time

00:40:49.180 --> 00:40:51.580
to carefully think through
and make policies around this,

00:40:51.580 --> 00:40:52.665
but it's already here.

00:40:52.665 --> 00:40:54.040
And we don't have
those policies.

00:40:54.040 --> 00:40:55.780
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And even
then, without those policies,

00:40:55.780 --> 00:40:57.910
I mean, people are going to
play around with it, right?

00:40:57.910 --> 00:40:59.493
I mean, this whole
community is driven

00:40:59.493 --> 00:41:01.450
on open source in general.

00:41:01.450 --> 00:41:03.740
People are encouraged to
experiment and play around

00:41:03.740 --> 00:41:04.240
with things.

00:41:04.240 --> 00:41:04.660
JOHN BOHANNON: Absolutely.

00:41:04.660 --> 00:41:05.560
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And
so you naturally get

00:41:05.560 --> 00:41:08.080
people who are like, oh,
I wonder if I can do this.

00:41:08.080 --> 00:41:08.788
And then they do.

00:41:08.788 --> 00:41:11.372
And then they're like, hey, I'm
going to go talk to my friends

00:41:11.372 --> 00:41:12.070
about this.

00:41:12.070 --> 00:41:14.410
And then you kind of just
build this entire boulder

00:41:14.410 --> 00:41:16.562
that's kind of rolling
down a hill of like, wow,

00:41:16.562 --> 00:41:18.520
these are things that
are completely unchecked.

00:41:18.520 --> 00:41:20.350
And there's a lot of
kind of scary practice--

00:41:20.350 --> 00:41:22.330
I mean, I know we're being
really vague about what

00:41:22.330 --> 00:41:23.710
those details are,
but I think people

00:41:23.710 --> 00:41:24.910
can use their imaginations.

00:41:24.910 --> 00:41:27.230
There's a lot of really
scary stuff out there.

00:41:27.230 --> 00:41:29.980
I don't even know how you
would kind of put organization

00:41:29.980 --> 00:41:31.030
around it.

00:41:31.030 --> 00:41:31.600
JOHN BOHANNON:
Yeah, I mean, that's

00:41:31.600 --> 00:41:32.740
a whole policy discussion.

00:41:32.740 --> 00:41:35.050
But essentially, the
best idea I've heard

00:41:35.050 --> 00:41:38.170
is that you need to have
laws on the books that

00:41:38.170 --> 00:41:42.310
make real consequences for
hurting people by reputation

00:41:42.310 --> 00:41:44.720
or however it has
a negative impact.

00:41:44.720 --> 00:41:47.440
You have to have laws on the
books that have consequences.

00:41:47.440 --> 00:41:48.700
MICHELLE CASBON: And what I
really liked about that group

00:41:48.700 --> 00:41:51.033
is they weren't just sort of
getting together in a room,

00:41:51.033 --> 00:41:51.700
experimenting.

00:41:51.700 --> 00:41:54.130
We started out the workshop
with the discussion,

00:41:54.130 --> 00:41:56.230
and they asked some really
intriguing questions.

00:41:56.230 --> 00:41:59.020
It was people who maybe don't
understand the technology very

00:41:59.020 --> 00:42:00.460
well, trying to
understand it more

00:42:00.460 --> 00:42:03.100
and asking important questions
that people who use technology

00:42:03.100 --> 00:42:04.090
haven't thought of.

00:42:04.090 --> 00:42:06.580
It was that coming together
of those two domains

00:42:06.580 --> 00:42:09.280
that I found just
fascinating, that there

00:42:09.280 --> 00:42:11.080
was so much crossover
and interests

00:42:11.080 --> 00:42:12.375
and what can we achieve.

00:42:12.375 --> 00:42:14.500
That's where innovation
has happened traditionally,

00:42:14.500 --> 00:42:16.900
is when you got these two
intersecting fields that

00:42:16.900 --> 00:42:18.820
come together and
like, oh, we haven't

00:42:18.820 --> 00:42:21.270
looked at this problem from
that perspective before.

00:42:21.270 --> 00:42:22.062
JOHN BOHANNON: Yep.

00:42:22.062 --> 00:42:24.730
And what I love about
the event especially

00:42:24.730 --> 00:42:26.650
is that it's not just one-sided.

00:42:26.650 --> 00:42:29.860
It's not just naive people
from outside of science

00:42:29.860 --> 00:42:34.990
ask hard questions that inspire
scientists to better explain

00:42:34.990 --> 00:42:36.545
and think about their own stuff.

00:42:36.545 --> 00:42:37.670
It also goes the other way.

00:42:37.670 --> 00:42:40.150
So we had one night
not too long ago where

00:42:40.150 --> 00:42:42.850
we were playing with a new
piece of technology that

00:42:42.850 --> 00:42:45.430
senses your heartbeat
and transmits it

00:42:45.430 --> 00:42:48.760
via router to whatever
you want it to go to.

00:42:48.760 --> 00:42:50.350
And so we sent it
to, essentially,

00:42:50.350 --> 00:42:53.510
the sound system of the
room-- very straightforward.

00:42:53.510 --> 00:42:55.900
So you basically strap
this thing on to someone,

00:42:55.900 --> 00:42:59.150
and suddenly the entire room
is engulfed in their heartbeat.

00:42:59.150 --> 00:43:01.690
And so as they become
calm or excited,

00:43:01.690 --> 00:43:03.945
essentially, everyone
experiences it.

00:43:03.945 --> 00:43:07.120
And so it's kind of very
simple concept, well executed,

00:43:07.120 --> 00:43:10.740
and then we then just
switched over to dance mode,

00:43:10.740 --> 00:43:13.240
where we have some of the most
amazing people from the dance

00:43:13.240 --> 00:43:15.760
community in San
Francisco just there,

00:43:15.760 --> 00:43:17.140
helping to run this thing.

00:43:17.140 --> 00:43:20.140
And they know how to take a
room of people, even non-movers,

00:43:20.140 --> 00:43:22.000
people who aren't
used to being movers,

00:43:22.000 --> 00:43:24.227
and make it safe for
them to start moving.

00:43:24.227 --> 00:43:25.810
Suddenly the entire
room was basically

00:43:25.810 --> 00:43:28.843
this spontaneous dance event.

00:43:28.843 --> 00:43:30.010
And it was really beautiful.

00:43:30.010 --> 00:43:31.718
And people just came
up to me afterwards,

00:43:31.718 --> 00:43:34.580
who have never experienced
that, were like, that's amazing.

00:43:34.580 --> 00:43:36.610
Is this every Wednesday?

00:43:36.610 --> 00:43:37.932
And yeah, it's that easy.

00:43:37.932 --> 00:43:40.140
You just get people in the
room with good intentions,

00:43:40.140 --> 00:43:41.723
and you can do some
really cool stuff.

00:43:41.723 --> 00:43:43.473
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah,
that's fascinating.

00:43:43.473 --> 00:43:45.780
I love that there are so
many aspects to your journey,

00:43:45.780 --> 00:43:48.312
that you're not just someone
who's working on AI now,

00:43:48.312 --> 00:43:50.520
but you're a person with
these very rich experiences,

00:43:50.520 --> 00:43:51.785
this very rich background.

00:43:51.785 --> 00:43:53.160
Is there anything
else you'd like

00:43:53.160 --> 00:43:54.613
to share about your background?

00:43:54.613 --> 00:43:55.410
What was your--

00:43:55.410 --> 00:43:57.510
MARK MIRCHANDANI: We've gotten
a really big history of--

00:43:57.510 --> 00:43:57.960
MICHELLE CASBON: We have.

00:43:57.960 --> 00:43:58.800
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
--all the cool things.

00:43:58.800 --> 00:44:01.005
And I know that we've
probably gone a little long.

00:44:01.005 --> 00:44:03.360
It would definitely
be useful to ask,

00:44:03.360 --> 00:44:05.100
is there anywhere
you're going here soon?

00:44:05.100 --> 00:44:06.050
Or is there anywhere
you're going to be?

00:44:06.050 --> 00:44:08.270
Are there any cool dance
competitions coming up

00:44:08.270 --> 00:44:09.630
that people should find you at?

00:44:09.630 --> 00:44:11.160
JOHN BOHANNON: Well, let's see.

00:44:11.160 --> 00:44:12.577
The big thing in
my life right now

00:44:12.577 --> 00:44:14.160
is that the "Archive"
paper that we've

00:44:14.160 --> 00:44:16.300
been working on for a while
is coming out tomorrow.

00:44:16.300 --> 00:44:18.037
And let's see, beyond that--

00:44:18.037 --> 00:44:19.620
MICHELLE CASBON: I
did want to ask you

00:44:19.620 --> 00:44:21.940
what your favorite job was.

00:44:21.940 --> 00:44:24.240
JOHN BOHANNON: Oh.

00:44:24.240 --> 00:44:26.460
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I mean,
was it the saving the fish?

00:44:26.460 --> 00:44:29.233
Was it working with the military
and getting the numbers?

00:44:29.233 --> 00:44:30.150
Was it being a journ--

00:44:30.150 --> 00:44:32.020
I mean, there are a lot of
jobs you've had, clearly.

00:44:32.020 --> 00:44:33.510
MICHELLE CASBON: And I don't
think that's the full list.

00:44:33.510 --> 00:44:33.760
What haven't you told us about?

00:44:33.760 --> 00:44:35.370
MARK MIRCHANDANI: No, I'm
sure it's the truncated.

00:44:35.370 --> 00:44:37.787
JOHN BOHANNON: I think there's
a special place in my heart

00:44:37.787 --> 00:44:41.760
still for the year after
graduating from undergrad

00:44:41.760 --> 00:44:46.410
before grad school, where I was
a nude model for art classes

00:44:46.410 --> 00:44:48.800
at Hammersmith
and Mount Holyoke,

00:44:48.800 --> 00:44:51.300
simply because it was pretty
well-paying at the time for me,

00:44:51.300 --> 00:44:53.790
and they didn't
have enough males.

00:44:53.790 --> 00:44:56.160
And I did it just
for life experience,

00:44:56.160 --> 00:44:58.740
but actually, now
that I've done yoga,

00:44:58.740 --> 00:45:01.120
I actually know what
I was tapping into.

00:45:01.120 --> 00:45:04.110
But it was like my first
experience with meditation

00:45:04.110 --> 00:45:06.240
and in a very unusual setting.

00:45:06.240 --> 00:45:08.215
And I got paid to
meditate, basically.

00:45:08.215 --> 00:45:09.840
MICHELLE CASBON: So
you were meditating

00:45:09.840 --> 00:45:11.708
while people painted you?

00:45:11.708 --> 00:45:13.500
JOHN BOHANNON: The best
time in that period

00:45:13.500 --> 00:45:15.540
was working for one sculptor.

00:45:15.540 --> 00:45:20.580
So I had to hold the same pose
for week after week after week.

00:45:20.580 --> 00:45:25.417
And so it just whittles away
your awareness of your body.

00:45:25.417 --> 00:45:27.000
At the same time,
it hyperconcentrates

00:45:27.000 --> 00:45:27.630
your awareness of the body.

00:45:27.630 --> 00:45:28.713
It's very hard to explain.

00:45:28.713 --> 00:45:31.860
But I really have a very
sentimental recollection

00:45:31.860 --> 00:45:32.712
of that job.

00:45:32.712 --> 00:45:34.170
MICHELLE CASBON:
Of all the lessons

00:45:34.170 --> 00:45:36.328
I expected to hear
from someone who

00:45:36.328 --> 00:45:38.370
has experience as a nude
model, that was probably

00:45:38.370 --> 00:45:40.390
the last one I expected to hear.

00:45:40.390 --> 00:45:41.680
And I think it's amazing.

00:45:41.680 --> 00:45:42.720
JOHN BOHANNON: What
did you expect to hear?

00:45:42.720 --> 00:45:43.320
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
I'll cut it off

00:45:43.320 --> 00:45:44.695
right there,
because I'm not sure

