00:47:29.240 --> 00:47:33.140
to build an AutoML natural
language classifier.

00:47:33.140 --> 00:47:34.640
And the beautiful
part about AutoML,

00:47:34.640 --> 00:47:36.057
if you're not
familiar with it, is

00:47:36.057 --> 00:47:38.570
it lets you build machine
learning models without really

00:47:38.570 --> 00:47:39.740
writing any code, right?

00:47:39.740 --> 00:47:41.180
So you kind of
send the data, you

00:47:41.180 --> 00:47:43.140
classify what that
data looks like,

00:47:43.140 --> 00:47:45.740
and then it trains a machine
learning model for you.

00:47:45.740 --> 00:47:47.640
That, in itself, is magic.

00:47:47.640 --> 00:47:49.670
But this walks through
how do you actually

00:47:49.670 --> 00:47:51.170
put the magic in your hands?

00:47:51.170 --> 00:47:52.520
How do you build that out?

00:47:52.520 --> 00:47:54.740
How do you go from
here's a set of data,

00:47:54.740 --> 00:47:56.900
to here's a working
machine learning prediction

00:47:56.900 --> 00:47:59.460
model in actually a pretty
short period of time?

00:47:59.460 --> 00:48:01.220
I walked through it,
and I was shocked

00:48:01.220 --> 00:48:02.973
at how quick you
could kind of just

00:48:02.973 --> 00:48:04.140
build this entire thing out.

00:48:04.140 --> 00:48:05.930
I think it's always
surprising to see

00:48:05.930 --> 00:48:07.550
all of the machine
learning in action.

00:48:07.550 --> 00:48:09.790
And I don't know a lot
about machine learning.

00:48:09.790 --> 00:48:12.577
So it's fascinating to see how
easy it is to dive in there.

00:48:12.577 --> 00:48:14.660
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah,
AutoML, in particular, it's

00:48:14.660 --> 00:48:17.420
really interesting the way
they've built the service.

00:48:17.420 --> 00:48:19.830
So it uses something
called transfer learning.

00:48:19.830 --> 00:48:23.150
So the service trains a bunch
of models behind the scenes.

00:48:23.150 --> 00:48:24.650
And then you come
in with your data,

00:48:24.650 --> 00:48:26.720
and it sort of adds
your specific use case

00:48:26.720 --> 00:48:28.880
on top of the models that
have already been built.

00:48:28.880 --> 00:48:31.053
And it gives you a much
more personalized model.

00:48:31.053 --> 00:48:33.470
But it's still very accurate
because of how they built it.

00:48:33.470 --> 00:48:35.762
And it's a great way to get
started if you don't really

00:48:35.762 --> 00:48:37.995
know much about deep
learning because it's a UI.

00:48:37.995 --> 00:48:39.620
And you don't need
a whole lot of data,

00:48:39.620 --> 00:48:41.570
and you can leverage
a lot of the hard work

00:48:41.570 --> 00:48:44.070
by people who know a lot more
about deep learning than I do.

